import strings.String

//# The `Debug` interface: a value as a developer reads it, what `dbg`,
//# `io.inspect` and an assertion's `values:` row write. Every value has a
//# `Debug` form; `Display` (`std::display`) is the user-facing one.
//#
//# ## How built-in values render
//#
//# | Type | Display | Debug |
//# |---|---|---|
//# | `Int`, `Float`, `Bool` | numeric / variant form: `42`, `3.14`, `True` / `False` | identical |
//# | `Decimal` | its digits at its scale: `1.50` | its literal: `1.50d` |
//# | `Unit` | no Display impl; rendering one is a compile error | `Unit` |
//# | `String` | raw text, no quotes | its literal, `String.to_literal`: `"..."` with `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\\`, `\"` and `\${` escaped and `\u{...}` for any other character that is not printable |
//# | `Infallible` | uninhabited; can never be called | none |
//#
//# `Int`, `Float` and `Bool` render the same in both, and a `Decimal`'s
//# Debug adds its literal's `d`. For a String the two differ more. Display
//# gives the bare text, so `"hello ${name}"` doesn't double-quote. Debug
//# gives the literal, so `io.inspect("a\"b")` shows the escape;
//# `String.to_literal` gives the rules for that literal.
//#
//# Containers recurse through the same interface as the caller: a container's
//# Display uses Display on its elements, and its Debug uses Debug. Brackets,
//# braces, parentheses and separators are byte-identical between the two;
//# only the per-element function differs.
//#
//# | Type | Display | Debug |
//# |---|---|---|
//# | `List<T>` | `[Display(v1), Display(v2), ...]`, `[]` for empty | `[Debug(...), ...]` |
//# | `Vector<T>` | `Vector[Display(v1), ...]`, `Vector[]` for empty | `Vector[Debug(...), ...]` |
//# | `Set<T>` | `Set[Display(v1), ...]`, `Set[]` for empty | `Set[Debug(...), ...]` |
//# | `Map<K, V>` | `{Display(k) => Display(v), ...}`, `{}` for empty | `{Debug(...) => Debug(...), ...}` |
//# | `Maybe<T>` | `Some(Display(v))`, `None` | `Some(Debug(v))`, `None` |
//# | `Result<T, E>` | `Ok(Display(v))`, `Err(Display(e))` | `Ok(Debug(v))`, `Err(Debug(e))` |
//# | `Range<T>` | `Debug(start)..Debug(end)`, `Debug(start)..=Debug(end)`, `Debug(start)..` for unbounded integer ranges | identical |
//# | Tuple `(...)` | `(Display(a), Display(b), ...)` | `(Debug(a), Debug(b), ...)` |
//# | Anon struct `{f: V, ...}` | `{f: Display(v), ...}` | `{f: Debug(v), ...}` |
//#
//# Tuples and anonymous structs are syntactic intrinsics with no named type
//# to attach an impl to; they get language-level rendering, as their literal
//# syntax is privileged. Every other built-in type gets a Nomi-side impl in
//# the standard library.
//#
//# Container impls require their elements to implement the corresponding
//# interface: `${[some_fn]}` is an error because functions have no `Display`
//# impl. Each container impl states the constraint as a bound
//# (`impl Display for List<T> where T: Display`), which its body needs to
//# render an element, so `List<Foo>` is Display exactly when `Foo` is; a
//# missing element impl is reported as the element's
//# (``no impl of `Display` for `Foo` ``).
//#
//# For `Maybe` and `Result`, the variant constructor is always rendered
//# (`Some(...)`, `None`, `Ok(...)`, `Err(...)`), never unwrapped. Display
//# keeps the variant: the user-facing form of `Maybe.None` is `"None"`, not
//# the empty string.

/// Protocol for a developer-facing `String` form of a value.
///
/// An impl writes text that looks like the value's literal: Nomi source
/// that builds the value again where that is possible, and a faithful
/// rendering of its structure where it is not.
///
/// `io.inspect`, `dbg`, debug logs and `Debug.inspect(value)` write it.
/// Text for a user is `Display`'s: string interpolation `"${value}"` calls
/// `Display`, not `Debug`.
///
/// `dbg expr` is a language form, not a member of this interface. It evaluates
/// `expr`, prints the source location, source text, and `Debug.inspect` output,
/// then returns the original value. The LSP reports every `dbg` as a warning
/// because it is for development only. `io.inspect(value)` is the function
/// for printing the Debug form as part of a program's behavior.
///
/// Debug's function is `inspect` and Display's is `to_string`, so a type
/// that implements both has two functions with two names.
///
/// For a `String` the two differ: `Display.to_string("hello")` is `hello`,
/// and `Debug.inspect("hello")` is `"hello"`, in quotes as the literal is.
/// For `Int`, `Float`, and `Bool`, `Debug.inspect` is the same text as
/// `Display.to_string`, because the literal is the user-facing form.
///
/// Every value has a `Debug` form, with no `derive` and no impl. The
/// compiler writes a structural `Debug` for any declared type that does not
/// supply one, so an `impl Debug` is only for changing how a type renders.
///
/// Precedence, highest first: a hand-written `impl Debug`, then `derive
/// Debug`, then the structural impl the compiler writes.
///
/// An opaque type inspects as its name only, `<opaque T>`, since a
/// structural form would show the internals `opaque` hides; write an `impl
/// Debug for T` in its file to show more. A host type with no impl
/// inspects as its bare name (`True` as `True`, `Unit` as `Unit`). Values
/// with no declaration have placeholders: `<function>`, `<task>`,
/// `<channel>`, `<arm>` and `<iter>`.
pub interface Debug
    fn inspect(value: self) -> String
end
